vkd3d: Requiem clamps the wave size in most places, and the rest is the bug.

**docs/TODO.md #56, and this is the mechanism the entry never had.** Five
compute shaders were dumped from the broken scene and disassembled. Three are
`LocalSize 32 1 1`, and they read `WaveGetLaneCount()` six times: three reads go
through `UMin(32, SubgroupSize)`, and the load-bearing ones do not.

    %402 = OpLoad %uint %SubgroupSize        ; raw
    %403 = OpIMul %uint %402 %401            ; base   = waveSize * groupIdx
    %510 = OpShiftLeftLogical %uint %402 %508
    %513 = OpIAdd %uint %509 %138            ; + WavePrefixCountBits(true), 0..31
    %540 = OpIAdd %uint %539 %402            ; stride by waveSize

The shader means wave size to equal the group size. **On this device it is
double that.** `threadsize_base` defaults to 64 (`freedreno_dev_info.py:108`)
and neither `a7xx_base` nor `a8xx_base` overrides it, so Turnip reports
`minSubgroupSize == maxSubgroupSize == 64`. Lane ranks then cover 0..31 of a
window advanced by 64, and `ceil(count / 64)` dispatches half the groups:
**exactly every second item is never processed.**

That is the symptom, precisely. It is deterministic, because it is arithmetic.
The objects still exist to the CPU, so collision holds against walls that are
never drawn. Nothing logs an error, because nothing is wrong -- the shader ran
and did what it was told. And only *some* geometry disappears, because the two
`LocalSize 128` shaders in the same set clamp to 128 and are correct for any
subgroup size that divides it. Desktop never sees it: NVIDIA reports 32, and
RADV picks wave32 for small compute groups.

vkd3d cannot fix it by asking for a narrower subgroup. The shaders carry no
`WaveSize` metadata, so `state.c` prepends no `requiredSubgroupSize`, and
`32 % 64 != 0` keeps `REQUIRE_FULL_SUBGROUPS` off as well.

**Upstream already fixed this exact bug for PRAGMATA, which is the same engine.**
Ten lines above the new entry:

    /* These shaders clamp the wave size to 32, but misses this in a few places
     * of course ... */
    static const struct vkd3d_shader_quirk_hash pragmata_hashes[] = {
        { "PersistentClusterCulling", 0,
          VKD3D_SHADER_QUIRK_CLAMP_WAVE_SIZE_TO_THREAD_GROUP32 | ... },

Requiem has no entry. This adds one.

**Global rather than a hash list, deliberately.** The quirk gates itself on the
workgroup being exactly 32 threads (`dxil_waveops.cpp`), wrapping each
`SubgroupSize` read in `UMin(SubgroupSize, num_threads)` -- so it reaches every
shader at risk and is inert for every other. A hash list would cover only the
five shaders that happened to be dumped, and those five were selected by an
unrelated ladder-merging warning, not by having this bug. There is no reason to
think they are the only ones.

**What this does not claim.** It has not been confirmed on the device yet, and
`ENABLE_FAIR_SCHEDULING` -- which PRAGMATA pairs with the clamp -- is not
included here, because whether these are persistent-thread loops that need it
has not been established. If the geometry returns but the title hangs or
starves, that pairing is the next thing to try.

diff --git a/libs/vkd3d/device.c b/libs/vkd3d/device.c
index ff9a03f1..90e22d74 100644
--- a/libs/vkd3d/device.c
+++ b/libs/vkd3d/device.c
@@ -1070,6 +1070,30 @@ static const struct vkd3d_shader_quirk_info pragmata_quirks = {
     pragmata_hashes, ARRAY_SIZE(pragmata_hashes), 0,
 };
 
+/* Resident Evil Requiem, and the same authoring mistake as PRAGMATA above.
+ *
+ * RE Engine's cluster-culling compute shaders clamp WaveGetLaneCount() to the
+ * threadgroup size in most places and use it raw in the rest. A 32-thread group
+ * then computes `base = waveSize * groupIdx` and strides by `waveSize` while
+ * only 32 lanes exist, so on a device reporting 64 every second window of work
+ * is skipped -- deterministically, with no error anywhere, and only for the
+ * 32-thread variants. The 128-thread ones clamp to 128 and are correct.
+ *
+ * Adreno reports minSubgroupSize == maxSubgroupSize == 64, and the shaders
+ * carry no WaveSize metadata, so vkd3d cannot ask for a narrower subgroup
+ * (state.c: no requiredSubgroupSize is prepended and 32 %% 64 != 0 keeps
+ * REQUIRE_FULL_SUBGROUPS off either). Desktop hides it -- NVIDIA reports 32
+ * and RADV picks wave32 for small compute groups.
+ *
+ * Global rather than a hash list on purpose. The quirk gates itself on the
+ * workgroup being exactly 32 threads (dxil_waveops.cpp), so it reaches every
+ * shader at risk and is inert for the rest -- where a hash list would only
+ * cover the handful anyone has happened to dump.
+ */
+static const struct vkd3d_shader_quirk_info re9_quirks = {
+    NULL, 0, VKD3D_SHADER_QUIRK_CLAMP_WAVE_SIZE_TO_THREAD_GROUP32,
+};
+
 static const struct vkd3d_shader_quirk_hash wow_hashes[] = {
     /* In addition to needing 64 byte descriptors, this ray query shader causes a heap OOB as well. */
     { NULL, 0xef2f620cbce5630c, VKD3D_SHADER_QUIRK_DESCRIPTOR_HEAP_ROBUSTNESS },
@@ -1193,6 +1217,8 @@ static const struct vkd3d_shader_quirk_meta application_shader_quirks[] = {
     { VKD3D_STRING_COMPARE_EXACT, "Spider-Man2.exe", &spiderman2_quirks },
     /* PRAGMATA (3357650) */
     { VKD3D_STRING_COMPARE_STARTS_WITH, "PRAGMATA", &pragmata_quirks },
+    /* Resident Evil Requiem */
+    { VKD3D_STRING_COMPARE_EXACT, "re9.exe", &re9_quirks },
     /* Forza Horizon 6 (2483190). */
     { VKD3D_STRING_COMPARE_EXACT, "forzahorizon6.exe", &forza6_quirks },
     /* World of Warcraft */
